In org "${SCRATCH_ORG}" ONLY, and READ-MOSTLY. Test the safe preflight path: connections.validateConnectorSource with (a) valid connector source and (b) deliberately broken source — confirm it returns {valid:false, diagnostics} WITHOUT persisting, exactly as documented. Test connections.getConnectorSource on an already-installed connector: does it return active version, code hash, retained version history? Test operations.listAvailable with connector_key filters and with a connection_id. Verify the documented claim that backend configuration is NEVER exposed in operations output — a leak of backend config/credentials is a BLOCKER. Do NOT install/uninstall/rollback a real connector unless you can fully reverse it; prefer validate-only. Report if the documented org-local guarantee cannot be verified.`,
  },
  {
    key: 'behaviors',
    prompt: `SLICE: Behaviors (the agent-facing name for watchers) — create/version/trigger.
In org "${SCRATCH_ORG}" ONLY. behaviors.list first. Then attempt the documented create path: behaviors.create requires slug, prompt, and agent_id — find a valid agent_id via the SDK. Create a Behavior named "mcp-e2e-...", create a version, trigger it, and observe whether the run actually executes and produces a window/result. Test the documented sources[] contract (each entry needs \`name\` + a read-only SELECT/WITH query projecting an \`id\` column) — verify a source violating that is rejected clearly. Test keying_config.entity_type. Clean up: behaviors.delete archives (soft) — that is the documented behavior, confirm it.
CRITICAL VOCABULARY CHECK: the agent-facing product name is "Behavior". The word "watcher" must NOT appear in MCP tool schemas, names, descriptions, or discovery metadata. Grep everything these tools return you for "watcher" leaking into agent-facing text and report each leak as a finding.`,
  },
  {
    key: 'metrics-schedules',
    prompt: `SLICE: metrics + schedules + views.
Test the metric surface: client.metrics.list (declared governed metrics per entity type), client.metrics.query for a declared measure, client.metrics.series for sparkline time-series. Verify series bounds behave (sane time windows, no unbounded scans) and that column safety holds — try to inject a bad column/measure name and confirm it fails closed rather than executing arbitrary SQL (a successful injection is a BLOCKER). Test schedules: list existing schedules, inspect timezone/until/idempotency handling in what is returned. Test view/template reads if reachable. In "${SCRATCH_ORG}" only if you create anything; prefer read-only here.`,
  },
  {
    key: 'authz-scoping',
    prompt: `SLICE: AUTHORIZATION + ORG SCOPING — the security slice. Fail-closed is the requirement.
You are a member of some orgs and NOT others (organizations.list reports is_member). Systematically attempt to read AND write data in orgs where is_member is FALSE (e.g. delivery, ecommerce, finance, leadership, legal-review, sales, support) using every available lever: query_sql with org_slug redirect, run_sdk/query_sdk methods that accept an org argument, entity ids belonging to another org, connection ids from another org, knowledge.read with content_ids from another org.
DO NOT actually mutate another org's data — you are testing that the attempt is REFUSED. If a write path appears to be permitted, STOP before completing the mutation and report it immediately as a blocker with the exact call.
