  },
  {
    key: 'errors-and-limits',
    prompt: `SLICE: error quality, limits, and agent-strandability.
This slice asks: when an agent misuses these tools, does it get unstuck? Systematically send WRONG input to every one of the 6 agent tools: wrong arg names, wrong types, missing required args, absurd limits (limit=999999, negative offset), oversized payloads, malformed scripts, unicode/emoji/injection-looking strings, null/empty values. For each, judge: is the error message ACTIONABLE (says what was wrong AND how to fix it) or would it strand an agent in a retry loop?
Specifically verify these documented-but-easy-to-miss traps produce clear errors: query_sdk/run_sdk requiring \`export default async function\` wrapping; query_sql rejecting positional params ($1); query_sql arg named \`sql\` not \`query\`; entities.search positional-only signature. Note that tool DESCRIPTIONS not warning about a trap that bites is itself a finding. Also test very large result handling — does the tool paginate/truncate gracefully or blow up?`,
  },
]

phase('Probe')
log(`Driving ${SLICES.length} MCP tool slices against PRODUCTION (writes confined to org "${SCRATCH_ORG}")`)

const results = await parallel(
  SLICES.map((s) => () =>
    agent(`${PREAMBLE}\n\n${s.prompt}`, {
      label: `probe:${s.key}`,
      phase: 'Probe',
      schema: FINDINGS_SCHEMA,
    })
  )
)

const good = results.filter(Boolean)
const allFindings = good.flatMap((r) =>
  (r.findings || []).map((f) => ({ ...f, slice: r.slice || 'unknown' }))
)

log(`${good.length}/${SLICES.length} slices reported; ${allFindings.length} raw findings to verify`)

if (allFindings.length === 0) {
  return {
    verdict: 'no findings surfaced',
    slices: good.map((r) => ({ slice: r.slice, calls: r.calls_made, worked: r.worked })),
  }
}

phase('Verify')

const VERDICT_SCHEMA = {
  type: 'object',
  properties: {
    real: { type: 'boolean', description: 'true only if you REPRODUCED it live against prod' },
    reproduced: { type: 'boolean' },
    what_you_observed: { type: 'string' },
    corrected_severity: { type: 'string', enum: ['blocker', 'major', 'minor', 'not-a-bug'] },
    reason: { type: 'string' },
  },
  required: ['real', 'reproduced', 'what_you_observed', 'corrected_severity', 'reason'],
}

const verified = await parallel(
  allFindings.map((f) => () =>
    agent(
      `${PREAMBLE}

You are an ADVERSARIAL VERIFIER. Another agent claims it found this defect in PRODUCTION. Your default assumption is that it is WRONG — most reported findings are call-shape mistakes by the reporter, or working-as-designed behavior the reporter misread.

CLAIM: ${f.title}
SEVERITY CLAIMED: ${f.severity}
TOOL: ${f.tool}
CALL THEY MADE: ${f.exact_call}
RESPONSE THEY SAW: ${f.exact_response}
WHY THEY SAY IT MATTERS: ${f.why_it_matters}

REPRODUCE IT YOURSELF with live tool calls. Then decide:
- Did you actually reproduce it? If you cannot reproduce it live, real=false.
- Is it truly a defect, or did the reporter simply call the tool wrong? Re-read the tool's own description/schema via search_sdk or the inputSchema before concluding. A tool correctly rejecting bad input is NOT a bug. A documented behavior the reporter didn't read is NOT a bug.
- Is the severity honest? A cosmetic error-message nit is 'minor', not 'blocker'. Reserve 'blocker' for: data loss, cross-org leak, accepted unauthorized write, security bypass, or a core documented flow that is completely broken.

Set real=true ONLY if you reproduced it AND it is a genuine defect. Be strict — a false positive here wastes the user's engineering time.`,
      { label: `verify:${f.slice}`, phase: 'Verify', schema: VERDICT_SCHEMA }
    ).then((v) => (v ? { ...f, verdict: v } : null))
  )
)

const confirmed = verified
  .filter(Boolean)
  .filter((f) => f.verdict.real && f.verdict.corrected_severity !== 'not-a-bug')

log(`${confirmed.length}/${allFindings.length} findings survived adversarial verification`)

phase('Synthesize')

const summary = await agent(
  `${PREAMBLE}

You are writing the final PROD-READINESS VERDICT for the Lobu MCP tool surface. ${SLICES.length} agents drove every tool slice against production; each finding below already survived an independent adversarial reproduction pass.

WHAT WORKED (per slice):
${JSON.stringify(good.map((r) => ({ slice: r.slice, calls: r.calls_made, worked: r.worked, not_testable: r.not_testable })), null, 2)}

CONFIRMED DEFECTS:
${JSON.stringify(confirmed.map((f) => ({ slice: f.slice, title: f.title, severity: f.verdict.corrected_severity, tool: f.tool, observed: f.verdict.what_you_observed, why: f.why_it_matters })), null, 2)}

Write a tight engineering verdict:
1. Is this surface PROD READY? Answer plainly: yes / yes-with-caveats / no. Justify in two sentences.
2. Blockers that must be fixed before shipping (if none, say so explicitly).
3. Major issues worth fixing soon.
4. Minor/polish.
5. COVERAGE HONESTY: what was NOT tested or not testable safely against prod, and what confidence that leaves. Be explicit — do not imply coverage that did not happen.
Be concise and specific. Cite the tool name for each item. No filler.`,
  { label: 'synthesize', phase: 'Synthesize' }
)

return {
  slices_run: good.length,
  total_calls: good.reduce((n, r) => n + (r.calls_made || 0), 0),
  raw_findings: allFindings.length,
  confirmed: confirmed.length,
  blockers: confirmed.filter((f) => f.verdict.corrected_severity === 'blocker').length,
  confirmed_findings: confirmed.map((f) => ({
    slice: f.slice,
    title: f.title,
    severity: f.verdict.corrected_severity,
    tool: f.tool,
    observed: f.verdict.what_you_observed,
  })),
  cleanup: good.map((r) => ({ slice: r.slice, cleanup: r.cleanup_done })),
  verdict: summary,
}

